<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">The fly-tip script</h3>

<div class="govuk-inset-text">
  <p class="govuk-body">Dial the number again. When the bot greets you, say:</p>
  <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-font-style-italic">
    "There's a load of rubbish been dumped in the alley behind my house. It's been there since the weekend. Mattresses, bin bags, the lot."
  </p>
</div>

<p class="govuk-body">The bot says something like:</p>

<div class="govuk-inset-text">
  <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-font-style-italic">
    "Thanks for reporting that. Could you send a photo? You can upload one right now through the WhatsApp, iMessage or RCS simulator on the right of the companion app. I'll wait."
  </p>
</div>

<p class="govuk-body">The line holds. The contact flow has dropped you into a long-poll branch that waits for the photo. You don't hang up; you upload.</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Download a photo to use</h3>

<div class="ndx-photo-download">
  <a href="/assets/downloads/sample-flytip.jpg" download class="govuk-button govuk-button--secondary">
    Download a sample fly-tip photo (JPEG, 440 KB)
  </a>
  <p class="govuk-body-s govuk-!-margin-top-2">
    A real-world picture of dumped rubbish you can send through the simulator. Or use any photo on your own device, the bot will describe whatever it sees.
  </p>
</div>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Open the simulator and send the photo</h3>

<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number govuk-list--spaced">
  <li>In the companion app, look at the <strong>right-hand pane</strong>. That's the WhatsApp/iMessage/RCS simulator. It's a stand-in for whichever messaging channel the resident actually uses.</li>
  <li>In the sender-phone field, enter the same number you dialled from in step 1. Use UK E.164 (<code>+44</code> followed by 10 digits) or local format (<code>0</code> followed by 10 digits). Cases looks up the open ticket by phone number.</li>
  <li>Click "Choose photo" and pick the sample fly-tip photo above (or any photo on your device).</li>
  <li>Click "Send".</li>
</ol>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">What you'll see</h3>

<p class="govuk-body">A staged sequence of chat bubbles appears in the simulator pane:</p>

<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
  <li><strong>~1 second:</strong> "Photo received. Sender ID: {your phone}"</li>
  <li><strong>~3 seconds:</strong> "Analysing image with Amazon Bedrock&hellip;" with a shimmer animation</li>
  <li><strong>~6 seconds:</strong> "Looking up your case in Connect Cases&hellip;"</li>
  <li><strong>~10 seconds:</strong> A structured response with <code>object_class</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>suggested_council_action</code>, <code>confidence</code>, and optional <code>secondary_observations</code></li>
</ul>

<blockquote class="govuk-body govuk-!-font-size-36 ndx-headline-moment">
  "I've reviewed your photo; environmental health will visit Wednesday, ref ABC123."
</blockquote>

<p class="govuk-body">That single sentence is the headline moment of the scenario. It's what a council CIO will remember and what a Director of Customer Services will photograph for their own CIO. Voice and a photo, joined to one case, read back as one promise.</p>

<p class="govuk-body">Behind the scenes, the web app calls <code>POST /api/upload-presign</code> on the companion API. The browser uploads the image directly to S3 using a presigned URL. Then the web app calls <code>POST /api/simulator/send</code> with the S3 key and your phone number, and a Lambda function handles the cross-channel link to your existing case.</p>

